menu "Xiaozhi Assistant"

config USE_EMOTE_MESSAGE_STYLE
    bool "Use rawdraw 1bpp rendering (no LVGL)"
    default y
    help
        When enabled, the display uses rawdraw 1bpp framebuffer rendering
        instead of LVGL. This is the recommended mode for the ZecTrix
        S3 E-Paper 4.2 board as LVGL causes frequent crashes.

config BOARD_TYPE_ZECTRIX_S3_EPAPER_4_2
    bool "zectrix-s3-epaper-4.2 FT firmware"
    default y
    depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3
    help
        This local repository has been reduced to a single FT-only firmware
        for the zectrix-s3-epaper-4.2 board.

choice ZECTRIX_EPD_PANEL_TYPE
    prompt "ZecTrix EPD panel type"
    default ZECTRIX_EPD_PANEL_4COLOR_SSD2683
    depends on BOARD_TYPE_ZECTRIX_S3_EPAPER_4_2
    help
        Select the e-paper panel connected to the board. The 4-color option
        keeps a semantic 2bpp BWRY UI framebuffer and sends the SSD2683 2bpp
        wire format during refresh. The 1bpp panel build down-converts it.

config ZECTRIX_EPD_PANEL_1BPP
    bool "1bpp black/white EPD"

config ZECTRIX_EPD_PANEL_4COLOR_SSD2683
    bool "4-color BWRY SSD2683 EPD"

endchoice

config ZECTRIX_EPD_4COLOR_BOOT_TEST_PATTERN
    bool "Show 4-color EPD test pattern at boot"
    default n
    depends on ZECTRIX_EPD_PANEL_4COLOR_SSD2683
    help
        Show black/white/red/yellow vertical bars once during display
        initialization so a newly connected 4-color panel can be verified.

config ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW
    bool "Use SSD2683 OTP fast waveform for interactive B/W refresh"
    default y
    depends on ZECTRIX_EPD_PANEL_4COLOR_SSD2683
    help
        Use the SSD2683 fast-waveform selection sequence published for the
        400x300 GDEM042F86 controller path. Interactive framebuffer updates
        are reduced to black/white and use this waveform. A standard color
        refresh remains available and is automatically run after the idle
        timeout below. Disable this option if a panel batch does not contain
        a compatible fast waveform in OTP.

choice ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW_TIMING
    prompt "SSD2683 B/W preview timing"
    default ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW_TIMING_ULTRA
    depends on ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW
    help
        Select how aggressively the controller executes the known OTP fast
        waveform. The vendor profile is the published approximately 12-second
        mode. The other profiles use documented SSD2683 PLL/CDI controls and
        are deliberately experimental: they trade settling time and image
        quality for interactive latency, while the standard full-color path
        remains unchanged as a recovery path.

config ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW_TIMING_VENDOR
    bool "Vendor timing: dynamic 12.5 Hz, 20 ms blanking"
    help
        Use PLL=0x08 and CDI=0x37 exactly as published by Good Display. This
        is the conservative fallback, but normally takes about 12 seconds.

config ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW_TIMING_120HZ
    bool "Experimental: fixed 120 Hz, 20 ms blanking"
    help
        Force the documented maximum 120 Hz scan rate while keeping the
        vendor 20 ms VCOM/data blanking interval. Use this to isolate PLL
        behavior from the more aggressive blanking change.

config ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW_TIMING_ULTRA
    bool "ULTRA_BW: fixed 120 Hz, 2 ms blanking"
    help
        Force the documented maximum 120 Hz scan rate and minimum documented
        2 ms VCOM/data blanking interval. This is the default interactive
        preview experiment. Expect color loss, ghosting, reduced contrast and
        panel-dependent results; the idle full-color refresh restores the
        normal controller initialization and waveform.

endchoice

config ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW_IDLE_FULL_SECONDS
    int "Idle seconds before full-color recovery"
    range 5 3600
    default 60
    depends on ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW
    help
        Restart this timer after every interactive FAST_BW request. Once no
        new request has arrived for this many seconds, perform one standard
        full-screen four-color refresh to restore colors and reduce ghosting.

config ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW_CLEANUP_MS
    int "Idle ms before one complete black-and-white waveform"
    range 500 60000
    default 3000
    depends on ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW
    help
        Middle rung of the refresh ladder. Truncated waveforms give immediate
        feedback while keys are being pressed but leave ghosting behind; once
        input stops for this long, one complete black-and-white waveform runs
        to clean the image up, well before the minute-long wait for the
        full-color refresh. It is skipped when no truncated refresh happened,
        and it also restores the DC balance the truncated ones skipped.

config ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW_TRUNCATE_MS
    int "Cut the fast waveform short after this many ms (0 disables)"
    range 0 20000
    default 0
    depends on ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW
    help
        The panel's OTP holds only four-color waveforms. Their later phases
        move the yellow and red pigment and contribute nothing to
        black-and-white content, which is already legible after the first
        drive. Stopping the wait early and powering off turns the roughly
        11.5 s fast refresh into whatever value is set here.

        This breaks the waveform's DC balance on purpose. Accumulated DC bias
        is the known cause of permanent image sticking on e-paper, so it is
        bounded by ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW_TRUNCATE_MAX_STREAK and by the idle
        full-color recovery. Start high and lower it only as far as the image
        stays acceptable.

config ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW_TRUNCATE_MAX_STREAK
    int "Consecutive truncated waveforms before forcing a complete one"
    range 1 1000
    default 20
    depends on ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW
    help
        After this many truncated refreshes in a row, the next one waits for
        the waveform to finish, which restores the DC balance the truncated
        ones skipped. Lower is safer for the panel and costs one full-length
        refresh more often.

config ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW_TRUNCATE_SWEEP
    bool "Sweep the truncation deadline across successive fast refreshes"
    default n
    depends on ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW
    help
        Only a person looking at the panel can judge how short the waveform may
        be cut, so this ignores the fixed deadline and uses 3000, 2000, 1500,
        1000, 700 then 400 ms on successive interactive refreshes, logging the
        deadline each one ran with. Six ordinary interactions therefore show
        the whole range against real UI content. Disable once a value is
        chosen.

config ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW_PLL_SWEEP
    bool "Sweep PLL values across successive fast refreshes (diagnostic)"
    default y
    depends on ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW
    help
        Ignore the fixed PLL value of the selected timing profile and instead
        program a different candidate on every fast refresh, logging the
        resulting waveform BUSY time for each. Six consecutive interactive
        refreshes therefore produce a full PLL-versus-duration curve in one
        flashing cycle. If every value yields the same BUSY time, the frame
        count stored in the OTP waveform bank determines the refresh duration
        and no register setting can shorten it. Disable for normal builds.

config ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW_TSSET
    int "TSSET (0xE6) waveform temperature section for fast refresh"
    range 0 255
    default 25
    depends on ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW
    help
        Selects which temperature section of the OTP waveform the fast refresh
        executes. Measured on hardware at PLL=0x07: 0 C took 23550 ms, 10 C
        22700 ms, 25 C 11500 ms, 40 C 14100 ms, 50 C 14100 ms and the vendor
        demo's magic 0x5A 14050 ms, so 25 is both the default and the shortest
        waveform present in OTP.

        This pins the waveform to one temperature rather than following the
        panel's own sensor. In a cold room the pixels are then under-driven,
        which shows up as reduced contrast or ghosting in the black-and-white
        preview; the idle full-color refresh still uses the panel's real
        temperature and cleans that up.

config ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW_TSSET_SWEEP
    bool "Sweep TSSET values across successive fast refreshes (diagnostic)"
    default n
    depends on ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW
    help
        Program a different TSSET (0xE6) value on every fast refresh and log
        the resulting waveform BUSY time. TSSET selects the temperature section
        of the OTP waveform, and those sections have different frame counts, so
        this probes whether the OTP contains a shorter waveform at all. The PLL
        sweep showed the duration bottoming out near 14 s, which corresponds to
        roughly 1700 frames, i.e. a four-color waveform. If every TSSET value
        gives the same BUSY time, the OTP holds no shorter waveform and 14 s is
        the floor without replacing the LUT. Disable for normal builds.

config ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW_PSR_SWEEP
    bool "Sweep the first PSR byte across successive fast refreshes (diagnostic)"
    default n
    depends on ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW
    help
        PSR (0x00) is a documented, volatile register whose bits include the
        selection between an OTP waveform LUT and a LUT loaded from registers.
        This sweep programs a different first PSR byte on each fast refresh to
        locate that bit: selecting the register LUT without having uploaded one
        should collapse the waveform duration, because the controller then runs
        an empty waveform. Nothing persists, since every refresh begins with a
        hardware reset. Disable for normal builds.

config ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW_TSSET_SWEEP_BOOT
    bool "Run the active sweep once at boot"
    default n
    depends on ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW_TSSET_SWEEP || ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW_PSR_SWEEP || ZECTRIX_EPD_FAST_BW_PLL_SWEEP
    help
        Perform the whole TSSET sweep during display initialization instead of
        waiting for interactive refreshes. An untouched device never produces a
        dirty rect, so the interactive sweep cannot advance on its own. The
        screen alternates black and white once per sweep value, which takes a
        couple of minutes, then normal initialization resumes.

config ZECTRIX_EPD_READ_CLOCK_HZ
    int "SPI clock for EPD register reads (Hz)"
    range 100000 8000000
    default 1000000
    depends on ZECTRIX_EPD_PANEL_4COLOR_SSD2683
    help
        Controller reads are much slower than writes and use the shared SDIN
        line turned around. The first MTP dump was taken at the 8 MHz write
        clock and came back as recognisable data corrupted by bit slips, so
        reads now use this separate, lower clock. Lower it further if the dump
        reports UNSTABLE.

config ZECTRIX_EPD_SSD2683_MTP_DUMP
    bool "Dump SSD2683 revision and read-only MTP waveform data at boot"
    default n
    depends on ZECTRIX_EPD_PANEL_4COLOR_SSD2683
    help
        Read the three-byte chip revision and all 3840 bytes of MTP through
        the documented REV (0x70) and RMTP (0x92) commands. A fingerprint and
        complete hex dump are written to the serial log for waveform reverse
        engineering. This path never sends the irreversible PGM/APG commands
        (0x90/0x91). Leave disabled for normal builds because the dump is long.

endmenu
